#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""typist-run — runner for the fast-typist preregistration (2026-09-05).

Two arms over ONE frozen dossier (bin/typist-dossier.md, byte-identical to both):

  --arm N --runs 1   one careful author  (codex exec -m gpt-5.6-sol)
  --arm F --k 5      k parallel typists   (--provider groq|openrouter|spark)
  --arm fake --k 2   the ratchet: known-good + known-bad diffs, no network
  --replay <dir>     re-judge RETAINED candidate bytes; no network
  --bench            compare typists head to head; one table at the end

WHICH fast typist arm F buys is a provider choice, not a different arm: every
provider returns the same candidate record and every candidate goes through the
same apply / gate / acceptance judging.

  groq        Groq openai/gpt-oss-120b        (reports completion/queue time)
  openrouter  openai/gpt-oss-120b via OpenRouter, pinned to Cerebras then Groq
  spark       codex exec -m gpt-5.3-codex-spark, low reasoning; k = k processes

PROVIDER FENCE (2026-09-06, after the Sol fence review). Nothing about a live
provider call is caller-selectable:

  * KEY PATHS ARE FIXED — /home/forge/secrets/groq.edn and
    /home/forge/secrets/openrouter.edn. No TYPIST_*_KEY_FILE, no env of any
    kind: an environment-selected path is an environment-selected key. The one
    seam is --keys-dir, refused unless it resolves under /var/tmp/forge/, and
    used only by the offline tests.
  * THE MODEL AND THE UPSTREAM ARE PINNED IN THE REGISTRY — there is no
    --model. Every OpenRouter request carries
    {"provider": {"order": ["Cerebras", "Groq"], "allow_fallbacks": false}}
    unconditionally, and a response routed anywhere else is the typed refusal
    `upstream-mismatch`, never a scored candidate.
  * ERRORS ARE TYPED, NEVER QUOTED — a provider exception can carry the
    Authorization header, so its text is never copied into a candidate record
    or a receipt. What is recorded is :error_class (http-4xx | http-5xx |
    timeout | transport | parse), :http_status, and one generic line. Every
    string written into an EDN receipt additionally passes through `scrub`.
  * The bench pre-flight checks that the key file EXISTS and is MODE 600. It
    never opens it; the key is read only inside the scope that builds one
    request.

  bin/typist-run --bench --providers groq,openrouter,spark \
                 --dossier bin/typist-dossier-onesite.md --rounds 6

--bench runs `rounds` rounds of k=1 per provider, INTERLEAVED round-robin so
every provider shares the same time-of-day weather, and writes the table to
/var/tmp/forge/typist-fx/bench-<epoch>.edn. A provider with no key, or one whose
calls all refuse, gets a row of refusals and "n/a" — never a fabricated time.

Every candidate diff is applied onto its OWN fresh copy of the fixture preimage,
then the GATE runs (the verification profile the mission ledger would resolve for
this workspace), then an INDEPENDENT ACCEPTANCE CHECK runs (a behavioural witness
that never loads the gate's test namespace, plus a byte-identity check of every
untouched file against the preimage manifest).

Clock: t0 is process start (startup is charged). Wall to first verified candidate
includes queue, transport, apply, gate and acceptance.

THE OFFLINE CONTRACT (TYPIST_OFFLINE=1) -- CHOKEPOINT, NOT SANDBOX.
Sol fence r4 is right and this is the corrected claim. TYPIST_OFFLINE

  * REFUSES THE RUNNER'S OWN spawn and connect paths at the chokepoint: every
    spawn and network entry point THIS process reaches through the standard
    library is wrapped at import (the table below), and a `codex`/`claude`
    shell word is refused as a string. Exit 6, typed, before the call.
  * DOES NOT CONFINE CHILDREN. It is NOT a sandbox. A general-purpose child
    (bash, python3) that this process is allowed to start can still spawn an
    absolute path, reset PATH, `env -i`, re-exec, or open a socket. Python
    monkeypatching plus a mutable PATH cannot make that impossible; only an
    OS-enforced process/network boundary or a strictly confined broker can.
  * IS UNAVAILABLE TO UPGRADE ON THIS BOX AS THIS USER: `unshare -rn` fails
    (write to uid_map not permitted), there is no bwrap and no firejail, and
    there is no sudo. So the guarantee the test suite actually offers is a
    WITNESSED one, not a confinement one -- see bin/typist-run-test:
      (a) NO REAL KEY IS REACHABLE -- the suite always runs the runner with
          --keys-dir pointed at a dummy-key directory under /var/tmp/forge,
          and proves it with `--print-key-paths` (paths only, never contents).
      (b) NOTHING WAS SPENT -- the ~/.codex/sessions PATH SET is identical
          before and after, and an egress canary shows no receipt written by
          the suite carries :cost_source "provider" or a real :upstream.
    A child COULD have spawned or connected; what is proven is that it DID
    NOT, and that it COULD NOT have spent.

Receipts written while TYPIST_OFFLINE=1 carry
:offline_contract "chokepoint-not-sandbox" so a later reader is never invited
to mistake this for confinement.

No number is ever fabricated: a missing API usage field prints `tokens=unknown`.
"""

import argparse
import atexit
import concurrent.futures as cf
import difflib
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request

T0 = time.time()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE OFFLINE CONTRACT -- CHOKEPOINT, NOT SANDBOX (Astra 2026-09-06; the name
# and the claim corrected after Sol fence r4, 2026-09-06).
#
# WHAT THIS IS: a refusal of the RUNNER'S OWN spawn and connect paths, at one
# chokepoint. WHAT THIS IS NOT: a sandbox. It does not confine children. Sol
# r4 demonstrated the escapes and they are real and unfixable at this layer --
# `bash -c /abs/path/codex`, a PATH reset, `env -i`, a re-exec from a spawned
# python3, a socket opened by a child. Python monkeypatching plus a mutable
# PATH is not a boundary; an OS-enforced process/network boundary or a
# strictly confined broker is, and NEITHER IS AVAILABLE on this box to this
# user (`unshare -rn` -> write to uid_map not permitted; no bwrap, no
# firejail, no sudo). Do not read the refusals below as containment.
#
# The guarantee the SUITE offers instead is witnessed, not enforced: no real
# key is reachable (--keys-dir dummies under /var/tmp/forge, proved with
# --print-key-paths) and nothing was spent (~/.codex/sessions path set
# unchanged; no provider-priced receipt written by the suite). See
# bin/typist-run-test. `bin/typist-run-test` calls itself an
# OFFLINE self-test, and it was one only by accident of geography: its NW
# "refused before any codex call" check pointed at the WORKTREE, and the write
# fence admits the worktree. On a checkout that lives inside /var/tmp/forge
# the fence therefore correctly ADMITTED the workspace, the refusal never
# fired, and the "offline" test made a real codex warm-up call -- 12.2 s and a
# created session. A test whose offline-ness is a property of where the repo
# happens to sit is not offline; it is lucky.
#
# So offline-ness becomes a state the program ENFORCES rather than a property
# the caller hopes for. TYPIST_OFFLINE is read ONCE, here, at import: this is
# a TEST SEAM, never a key or a policy dial -- it can only ever REFUSE more.
#
# The guard is installed at the CHOKEPOINT, not at each call site. There are
# four `codex exec` spawn sites and a `urlopen`, and a guard that must be
# remembered at each of them is a guard that is missing from the fifth.
#
# SOL FENCE R3, finding 3: the r2 guard was a guard on TWO NAMES, not a
# boundary. `subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True)`, a `bash -c` trampoline and
# `os.system` all walked straight past it, because each of those hands the
# command to a SHELL and the shell -- not this process -- does the exec. Two
# independent stops now close that, and each is listed here rather than
# remembered at a call site:
#
#   (a) THE CHOKEPOINT TABLE below -- every spawn and every network entry
#       point in the standard library that this process can reach is wrapped
#       at import, in one place. A shell STRING (os.system, os.popen, or any
#       subprocess call with shell=True) is scanned as a string, so
#       `codex exec ...` inside it is refused with the same exit 6.
#   (b) THE PATH SHIM -- under TYPIST_OFFLINE the runner prepends a temp dir
#       holding executable `codex` and `claude` scripts that print the typed
#       refusal and exit 6, so a shell string this process never sees the
#       inside of (`bash -c 'codex ...'`, an alias, a Makefile, a child of a
#       child) that resolves the NAME `codex` gets the refusal instead of the
#       real binary.
#
#       SOL FENCE R4 DEMONSTRATED ITS BYPASSES, and they are written here
#       rather than left as an impression: the shim is a NAME redirect on one
#       inherited variable, not a boundary. A child that invokes the ABSOLUTE
#       path, that rewrites or resets PATH, that runs under `env -i`, or that
#       re-execs through anything restoring the original environment reaches
#       the real binary. (a) fails loud in-process and (b) covers name lookups
#       (a) structurally cannot see -- NEITHER catches everything, and the
#       pair is not confinement. That is why the contract is named
#       chokepoint-not-sandbox and why the suite proves a WITNESSED guarantee
#       (no real key reachable; no session created; ledger did not grow)
#       instead of an impossibility claim.
#
# THE CHOKEPOINT TABLE (wrapped at import when OFFLINE):
#   os.system, os.popen
#   os.execl/execle/execlp/execv/execve/execvp/execvpe
#   os.spawnl/spawnle/spawnlp/spawnv/spawnve/spawnvp/spawnvpe, os.posix_spawn
#   subprocess.run, subprocess.call, subprocess.check_call,
#   subprocess.check_output, subprocess.Popen
#   urllib.request.urlopen, http.client.HTTPConnection/HTTPSConnection,
#   socket.create_connection
OFFLINE = os.environ.get("TYPIST_OFFLINE") == "1"

# The NAME of what TYPIST_OFFLINE provides, carried into every receipt written
# under it so no later reader mistakes a chokepoint for confinement.
OFFLINE_CONTRACT = "chokepoint-not-sandbox"
OFFLINE_CONTRACT_LINE = (
    "TYPIST_OFFLINE=1: refuses the runner's OWN spawn and connect paths at the "
    "chokepoint; it does NOT confine children; NOT a sandbox.")

OFFLINE_BLOCKED_EXES = ("codex", "claude")
# a blocked name as a WORD inside a shell string: `codex exec`, `/x/codex -m`,
# `FOO=1 codex`, `bash -c "claude ..."` -- but never `codexish` or `my-codex`.
OFFLINE_SHELL_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?:(?<=^)|(?<=[\s;&|(`]))(?:[\w./-]*/)?(%s)(?=$|[\s;&|)])"
    % "|".join(OFFLINE_BLOCKED_EXES))


def offline_refuse(what):
    """The typed offline refusal. Exit 6 -- its own class, distinct from the
    write fence's 4, so a test can tell "the fence stopped it" from "offline
    stopped it" without reading prose."""
    die("typist-run: refusing — offline: %s refused (TYPIST_OFFLINE=1)" % what, 6)


def _blocked_name(cmd, shell=False):
    """The blocked executable this command would start, or None.

    A LIST is judged by its argv[0] only -- `bash -c "codex ..."` is a bash
    spawn and is allowed to proceed straight into the PATH shim, which is the
    stop designed for it. A STRING is a shell command line, so every word in
    it is a candidate exec and the whole line is scanned."""
    if isinstance(cmd, (list, tuple)):
        if shell:
            return _blocked_name(" ".join(str(c) for c in cmd), shell=False)
        if not cmd:
            return None
        base = os.path.basename(str(cmd[0]))
        return base if base in OFFLINE_BLOCKED_EXES else None
    m = OFFLINE_SHELL_RE.search(str(cmd))
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def guard_spawn(cmd, shell=False):
    """Refuse a model-executor spawn BEFORE it happens."""
    if not OFFLINE:
        return
    name = _blocked_name(cmd, shell=shell)
    if name:
        offline_refuse("spawn of %s" % name)


def guard_network(what="network open"):
    if OFFLINE:
        offline_refuse(what)


def _install_offline_path_shim():
    """Prepend a PATH dir whose `codex` and `claude` print the typed refusal
    and exit 6. This is the stop for every route the in-process wrappers
    cannot see: a shell trampoline, an alias, a grandchild process."""
    import tempfile
    base = "/var/tmp/forge"
    d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="typist-offline-shim-",
                         dir=base if os.path.isdir(base) else None)
    for exe in OFFLINE_BLOCKED_EXES:
        p = os.path.join(d, exe)
        with open(p, "w") as fh:
            fh.write("#!/bin/sh\n"
                     "echo \"typist-run: refusing \u2014 offline: "
                     "spawn of %s refused (TYPIST_OFFLINE=1)\" >&2\n"
                     "exit 6\n" % exe)
        os.chmod(p, 0o755)
    os.environ["PATH"] = d + os.pathsep + os.environ.get("PATH", "")
    atexit.register(shutil.rmtree, d, True)
    return d


_real_subprocess_run = subprocess.run
_real_subprocess_call = subprocess.call
_real_subprocess_check_call = subprocess.check_call
_real_subprocess_check_output = subprocess.check_output
_real_subprocess_popen = subprocess.Popen
_real_urlopen = urllib.request.urlopen
_real_os_system = os.system
_real_os_popen = os.popen
_real_socket_create_connection = socket.create_connection

_OS_EXEC_SPAWN = ("execl", "execle", "execlp", "execv", "execve", "execvp",
                  "execvpe", "spawnl", "spawnle", "spawnlp", "spawnv",
                  "spawnve", "spawnvp", "spawnvpe", "posix_spawn",
                  "posix_spawnp")


def _guarded_run(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=kwargs.get("shell", False))
    return _real_subprocess_run(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_call(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=kwargs.get("shell", False))
    return _real_subprocess_call(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_check_call(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=kwargs.get("shell", False))
    return _real_subprocess_check_call(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_check_output(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=kwargs.get("shell", False))
    return _real_subprocess_check_output(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


class _GuardedPopen(_real_subprocess_popen):
    def __init__(self, cmd, *args, **kwargs):
        guard_spawn(cmd, shell=kwargs.get("shell", False)
                    or (len(args) >= 7 and args[6]))
        super().__init__(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_urlopen(req, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_network()
    return _real_urlopen(req, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_os_system(cmd):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=True)
    return _real_os_system(cmd)


def _guarded_os_popen(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
    guard_spawn(cmd, shell=True)
    return _real_os_popen(cmd, *args, **kwargs)


def _guarded_create_connection(*args, **kwargs):
    guard_network()
    return _real_socket_create_connection(*args, **kwargs)


def _guard_os_exec_family():
    """os.exec*/os.spawn* take the program as their FIRST argument in every
    spelling, so one wrapper shape covers the whole family."""
    def wrap(name, fn):
        def guarded(path, *args, **kwargs):
            guard_spawn([path], shell=False)
            return fn(path, *args, **kwargs)
        guarded.__name__ = name
        return guarded
    for name in _OS_EXEC_SPAWN:
        fn = getattr(os, name, None)
        if fn is not None:
            setattr(os, name, wrap(name, fn))


def _guard_http_client():
    import http.client

    for cls_name in ("HTTPConnection", "HTTPSConnection"):
        cls = getattr(http.client, cls_name, None)
        if cls is None:
            continue
        real_init = cls.__init__

        def guarded_init(self, *args, _real=real_init, **kwargs):
            guard_network()
            return _real(self, *args, **kwargs)

        cls.__init__ = guarded_init


subprocess.run = _guarded_run
subprocess.call = _guarded_call
subprocess.check_call = _guarded_check_call
subprocess.check_output = _guarded_check_output
subprocess.Popen = _GuardedPopen
urllib.request.urlopen = _guarded_urlopen

OFFLINE_SHIM_DIR = None
if OFFLINE:
    os.system = _guarded_os_system
    os.popen = _guarded_os_popen
    socket.create_connection = _guarded_create_connection
    _guard_os_exec_family()
    _guard_http_client()
    OFFLINE_SHIM_DIR = _install_offline_path_shim()

FX = os.environ.get("TYPIST_FX", "/var/tmp/forge/typist-fx")
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
# ---- the secrets fence -------------------------------------------------
# FIXED paths. Never environment-selected: a caller-controlled path is a
# caller-controlled key. The only seam is --keys-dir, fenced to /var/tmp/forge.
GROQ_KEY_FILE = "/home/forge/secrets/groq.edn"
OPENROUTER_KEY_FILE = "/home/forge/secrets/openrouter.edn"
TEST_KEYS_FENCE = "/var/tmp/forge"
KEYS_DIR = None            # set ONLY by --keys-dir, after the fence check
GROQ_URL = "https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions"
GROQ_MODEL = "openai/gpt-oss-120b"
GROQ_UA = "curl/8.5.0 forge-anvil-typist-run"   # urllib's default UA gets 403
GROQ_MAX_TOKENS = 6000   # reasoning tokens are spent first; budget covers both

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MISSIONS. Each dossier is paired with exactly one mission: its own preimage
# tree, its own manifest, its own verification profile, its own authorized file,
# and its own INDEPENDENT acceptance witness. The dossier is the prompt; the
# mission is everything the runner must know to judge a candidate for it.
#
# Adding a dossier without its mission is a refusal, not a default: judging a
# candidate against another mission's witness would be a silent wrong answer.

UTIL_CLJ = '''(ns fixture.util
  "Path helpers shared by the scope expander. NOT part of this mission."
  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))

(defn normalize
  "Trim a path and drop any trailing slash."
  [p]
  (str/replace (str/trim p) #"/+$" ""))
'''

SCOPE_CLJ = '''(ns fixture.scope
  "Scope expansion for the helper-extraction fence."
  (:require [fixture.util :as util]))

(def default-roots
  "The roots this workspace admits when the caller names none."
  ["src"])

(defn scope-roots
  "The admitted roots for this scope expansion."
  []
  default-roots)

(defn expand
  "Every admitted root plus the caller's paths, normalised, in order."
  [paths]
  (vec (distinct (map util/normalize (concat (scope-roots) paths)))))
'''

# ---- mission 1: scope-roots (cohort 1; a multi-line signature change) -----

SCOPE_SEED = {
"deps.edn": '{:paths ["src" "test"]}\n',

".clj-surgeon.edn": '''{:verification-profiles
 {"typist-narrow"
  {:commands [["bb" "-cp" "src:test" "-e"
               "(require 'clojure.test 'fixture.scope-test) (let [r (clojure.test/run-tests 'fixture.scope-test)] (System/exit (if (and (zero? (:fail r)) (zero? (:error r))) 0 1)))"]]}}}
''',

"src/fixture/util.clj": UTIL_CLJ,
"src/fixture/scope.clj": SCOPE_CLJ,

"test/fixture/scope_test.clj": '''(ns fixture.scope-test
  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]
            [fixture.scope :as scope]))

(deftest default-roots-unchanged
  (is (= ["src"] (scope/scope-roots))))

(deftest roots-opt-is-honoured
  (testing "(:roots opts) replaces the default roots"
    (is (= ["src" "test"] (scope/scope-roots {:roots ["src" "test"]})))
    (is (= ["lib"] (scope/scope-roots {:roots ["lib"]})))))

(deftest empty-roots-falls-back
  (is (= ["src"] (scope/scope-roots {:roots []}))))

(deftest expand-still-normalises
  (is (= ["src" "test"] (scope/expand ["test/"]))))
''',
}

# The independent acceptance witness. It requires ONLY fixture.scope — never the
# gate's test namespace — so a candidate that satisfies the gate by editing the
# tests cannot satisfy this.
SCOPE_ACCEPT = (
    "(require 'fixture.scope) "
    "(let [f (resolve 'fixture.scope/scope-roots)] "
    "(assert (= [\"src\"] (f)) \"0-arity default\") "
    "(assert (= [\"src\" \"test\"] (f {:roots [\"src\" \"test\"]})) \":roots honoured\") "
    "(assert (= [\"lib\"] (f {:roots [\"lib\"]})) \":roots honoured (single)\") "
    "(assert (= [\"src\"] (f {:roots []})) \"empty :roots falls back\") "
    "(assert (= [\"src\"] (f nil)) \"nil opts falls back\") "
    "(assert (vector? (f {:roots [\"a\"]})) \"returns a vector\") "
    "(println \"ACCEPT-OK\"))"
)

# ---- mission 2: onesite (the ONE-SITE CONTROL; a single literal) ----------
#
# The preregistration requires a control the careful author is expected to win
# or tie. This is it: one known site, one literal, no signature change, no
# call-site fan-out.

ONESITE_UTIL_CLJ = '''(ns fixture.util
  "Path helpers shared by the scope expander."
  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))

(def max-path-segments
  "The most path segments this workspace will normalise in one call."
  16)

(defn normalize
  "Trim a path and drop any trailing slash."
  [p]
  (str/replace (str/trim p) #"/+$" ""))
'''

ONESITE_SEED = {
"deps.edn": '{:paths ["src" "test"]}\n',

".clj-surgeon.edn": '''{:verification-profiles
 {"typist-onesite"
  {:commands [["bb" "-cp" "src:test" "-e"
               "(require 'clojure.test 'fixture.util-test) (let [r (clojure.test/run-tests 'fixture.util-test)] (System/exit (if (and (zero? (:fail r)) (zero? (:error r))) 0 1)))"]]}}}
''',

"src/fixture/util.clj": ONESITE_UTIL_CLJ,
"src/fixture/scope.clj": SCOPE_CLJ,

"test/fixture/util_test.clj": '''(ns fixture.util-test
  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]]
            [fixture.util :as util]))

(deftest max-path-segments-is-64
  (is (= 64 util/max-path-segments)))

(deftest normalize-unchanged
  (is (= "a/b" (util/normalize "  a/b//  "))))
''',
}

ONESITE_ACCEPT = (
    "(require 'fixture.util) "
    "(let [v @(resolve 'fixture.util/max-path-segments) "
    "      n (resolve 'fixture.util/normalize)] "
    "(assert (= 64 v) \"max-path-segments is 64\") "
    "(assert (integer? v) \"max-path-segments is an integer literal\") "
    "(assert (= \"a/b\" (n \"  a/b//  \")) \"normalize unchanged\") "
    "(assert (= \"\" (n \"   \")) \"normalize unchanged (blank)\") "
    "(println \"ACCEPT-OK\"))"
)

# ---- mission 3: fanout (the SEARCH mission; multi-file, multi-hunk) ------
#
# Cohort 1 found a single gpt-oss-120b candidate verifying 12/12 on both of the
# missions above, which makes k=5 unmeasurable: parallel search cannot beat a
# ceiling already reached at k=1. This mission is deliberately harder — six
# hunks across three files, a signature change fanned out to five call sites,
# and TWO traps a fast typist gets wrong: the old name survives inside a
# docstring example and inside a string literal, and must NOT be renamed.

FANOUT_UTIL_CLJ = r"""(ns fixture.util
  "Path helpers shared by the scope expander."
  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))

(defn normalize
  "Trim a path and drop any trailing slash."
  [p]
  (str/replace (str/trim p) #"/+$" ""))
"""

FANOUT_SCOPE_CLJ = r"""(ns fixture.scope
  "Scope expansion for the helper-extraction fence."
  (:require [fixture.util :as util]))

(def default-roots
  "The roots this workspace admits when the caller names none."
  ["src"])

(defn scope-roots
  "The admitted roots for this scope expansion."
  []
  default-roots)

(defn expand
  "Every admitted root plus the caller's paths, normalised, in order."
  [paths]
  (vec (distinct (map util/normalize (concat (scope-roots) paths)))))

(defn admits?
  "Does this scope admit the given path?"
  [p]
  (contains? (set (expand [])) (util/normalize p)))
"""

FANOUT_PATHS_CLJ = r"""(ns fixture.paths
  "Path assembly built on the shared helpers."
  (:require [clojure.string :as str]
            [fixture.util :as util]))

(def op-name
  "The operation name reported in telemetry."
  "normalize")

(defn join
  "Join segments into one path.

  Example:
    (normalize \"a/b/\")  ;=> \"a/b\""
  [segs]
  (util/normalize (str/join "/" segs)))

(defn relative-to
  "Strip the root prefix from a path."
  [root p]
  (let [r (util/normalize root)
        n (util/normalize p)]
    (if (str/starts-with? n (str r "/"))
      (subs n (inc (count r)))
      n)))

(defn clean-all
  "Normalise every path in the collection."
  [paths]
  (mapv util/normalize paths))
"""

FANOUT_TEST_CLJ = r"""(ns fixture.fanout-test
  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]
            [fixture.util :as util]
            [fixture.scope :as scope]
            [fixture.paths :as paths]))

(deftest normalize-path-renamed
